.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt STNS-KEY-WRAPPER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stns-key-wrapper
.Nd print a user's SSH public keys from an STNS directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
looks
.Ar user
up in the STNS API and writes their SSH public keys to standard output, one per
line.
It is meant to be used as
.Xr sshd_config 5 Ns 's
.Sy AuthorizedKeysCommand :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
AuthorizedKeysCommand     /usr/local/bin/stns-key-wrapper
AuthorizedKeysCommandUser nobody
.Ed
.Pp
This is the one part of STNS support that needs nothing at all from the
operating system's directory machinery -
.Xr sshd 8
runs a command and reads its output, and every system it runs on can do that.
So the same program is installed by
.Xr nss_stns 8
on
.Nx ,
.Fx
and
.Dx ,
by
.Xr ypstns 8
on
.Ox ,
and by
.Xr ldapstns 8
on macOS; it works identically on all of them, including on machines where
none of those is installed and the accounts are purely local.
.Pp
Configuration is read from
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
the same file the rest of STNS uses.
.Pp
.Nm
runs as the user named by
.Sy AuthorizedKeysCommandUser ,
which must therefore be able to read
.Xr stns.conf 5 .
Where that file holds a credential and must not be world readable, run
.Xr cache-stnsd 8
and point
.Sy [cached]
at its socket instead; the daemon then owns the credential and
.Nm
never needs it.
.Sh USING STNS FOR KEYS ALONE
It is a perfectly good arrangement to install this program and nothing else.
.Pp
Accounts are created locally, with
.Xr useradd 8
or
.Xr sysadminctl 8 ,
groups stay in
.Xr group 5 ,
and STNS supplies only the SSH keys.
No directory backend is configured at all - no
.Xr ypstns 8 ,
no
.Xr ldapstns 8 ,
no
.Xr nss_stns 8 -
so nothing on the machine can resolve a name that is not local, and only
accounts that exist locally can log in.
That is not a restriction that has to be enforced anywhere; it is simply what
happens when the only thing installed is a program
.Xr sshd 8
runs.
.Pp
.Xr sshd_config 5
puts local keys first:
.Sy AuthorizedKeysCommand
is tried after
.Sy AuthorizedKeysFile
and is not run at all if a matching key was found there.
So an existing
.Pa authorized_keys
keeps working, and a directory can be introduced one account at a time.
.Pp
Passwords are a different matter and this program is no help with them.
Verifying a local account's password is done against the hash in
.Xr master.passwd 5 ,
or on macOS against the local directory node, and having STNS answer that
question instead would need an authentication module - a
.Xr login.conf 5
style
.Pa /usr/libexec/auth/login_stns
on
.Ox ,
or a PAM module on macOS.
Neither exists.
Nor would either be straightforward: STNS deployments generally store SHA-512
crypt hashes, which macOS's
.Xr crypt 3
cannot verify at all and which
.Ox Ns 's
.Xr crypt_checkpass 3
handles only alongside its own preferred bcrypt.
Keys are the path that works.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 whenever it was able to answer at all, including when it has no keys to
print.
.Pp
That is deliberate and is not the same as "it worked".
.Xr sshd 8
runs this once per authentication attempt for every account on the machine,
local ones included, so a user the directory has never heard of is an ordinary
outcome and not an error.
The same goes for an API server that is unreachable: refusing to answer would
be no more secure, since
.Xr sshd 8
falls back to
.Pa authorized_keys
either way, and would turn a brief outage into an unreadable log.
.Pp
It exits 1 only when it could not have answered anybody: a
.Ar user
outside the character set STNS permits, or a configuration file that cannot be
read or parsed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check what
.Xr sshd 8
would be given, without involving
.Xr sshd 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
stns-key-wrapper alice
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sshd_config 5 ,
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
.Xr ldapstns 8 ,
.Xr sshd 8 ,
.Xr ypstns 8
.Pp
.Lk https://stns.jp/ "STNS"
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zakinko Aq Mt zakinko@snowrabbit.org
